= Bias–variance tradeoff — Simple =

The '''bias–variance tradeoff''' is one way to understand why a model can make errors.

'''Bias''' is systematic error: the model keeps missing important structure.

'''Variance''' is sensitivity: the model changes too much when the training sample changes.

Traditional textbooks often show a middle point between a model that is too rigid and one that is too sensitive.

That is useful, but modern large models do not always follow one simple U-shaped curve.

